A key architectural limitation of deploying DNSGuard Enterprise on serverless cloud platforms like Vercel is the reliance on a stateless, read-only file system, which prevents the web interface from persistently updating local configuration files such as rules.yaml during runtime. Because serverless containers are ephemeral and destroy local changes immediately after execution, any dynamic detection rule adjustments made via the SOC dashboard fail to persist across subsequent log uploads. To overcome this limitation in a production environment, the system's configuration management must be decoupled from the local filesystem and migrated to a centralized, persistent cloud database service—such as MongoDB, Firebase, or PostgreSQL. This architectural upgrade would enable the Flask API to perform stateful, real-time read and write operations, ensuring that custom detection thresholds and allowlists remain universally active and synchronized across all serverless function invocations.
